","The correct answer is (c) ZnO.  

**Explanation:**  
The reduction of metal oxide to metal by carbon involves the reaction between the oxide and carbon, where carbon is reduced and the metal is liberated. The reduction of an oxide by carbon is favored when the oxide has a high ionization energy, meaning the metal in the oxide is more stable in its oxidation state.  

For example, Fe₂O₃ can be reduced by carbon, yielding iron and CO₂:  
\[ \text{Fe}_2\text{O}_3 + 3\text{C} \rightarrow 2\text{Fe} + 3\text{CO}_2 \]  

Similarly, PO₄O₂ can be reduced by carbon:  
\[ \text{P}_4\text{O}_4 + 6\text{C} \rightarrow 4\text{P} + 2\text{CO}_2 \]  

However, ZnO is not reduced by carbon, as ZnO is stable at high temperatures.  

The correct reaction is:  
\[ \text{Zn} + 2\text{H}_2\text{O} \rightarrow \text{Zn}_2\text{O} + 2\text{H}_2 \]  

