","The equation of the line tangent to the graph of a differentiable function \( f \) at the point \( (a, f(a)) \) is given by:

\[
\frac{dy}{dx} \text{ at } x = a = \frac{dy}{dx} \text{ at } x = a = f'(a)
\]

The slope of the tangent line at \( x = a \) is the derivative of \( f \) at \( a \), which is \( f'(a) \). The line is tangent to the graph at \( (a, f(a)) \), so the line passes through this point. Using the equation of a line \( y = mx + b \), where \( m \) is the slope and \( b \) is the intercept, we can plug the slope \( f'(a) \) and the point \( (a, f(a)) \) into the equation to find the intercept \( b \):

\[
f(a) = f'(a)(a) + b
\]

Solving for \( b \):

\[
b = f(a) - af'(a)
\]

Thus, the equation of the line tangent to the graph of \( f \) at the point \( (a, f(a)) \) is:

\[
y = f'(a)(x - a) + f(a)
\]

This is the equation of the line tangent to the graph of \( f \) at \( x = a \).",relevance,"# Task Description
