","The Arrhenius equation is given by:
\[
k = A e^{-E_a/RT}
\]
where \( k \) is the rate constant, \( A \) is the pre-exponential factor, \( E_a \) is the activation energy, \( R \) is the gas constant, and \( T \) is the temperature.

Taking the natural logarithm of the Arrhenius equation:
\[
\ln k = \ln A - \frac{E_a}{RT}
\]

For two temperatures, the Arrhenius equation becomes:
\[
\ln k_1 = \ln A - \frac{E_a}{RT_1}
\]
\[
\ln k_2 = \ln A - \frac{E_a}{RT_2}
\]

Subtracting the first equation from the second:
\[
\ln k_2 - \ln k_1 = \left( \ln A - \frac{E_a}{RT_2} \right) - \left( \ln A - \frac{E_a}{RT_1} \right)
\]
\[
\ln \left( \frac{k_2}{k_1} \right) = \frac{E_a}{RT_1} - \frac{E_a}{RT_2}
\]
\[
\ln \left( \frac{k_2}{k_2} \right) = \frac{E_a}{RT} \left( \frac{1}{T_1} - \frac{1}{T_2} \right)
\]
\[
\ln \left( \frac{k_2}{R_1} \right) = \frac{E_a}{RT} \left( \frac{1}{T_1} - \frac{1}{T_2} \right)
\]

Solving for \( E_a \):
\[
E_a = \frac{R \ln \left( \frac{k_2}{k_1} \right)}{\frac{1}{T_1} - \frac{1}{T_2}}
\]

Substitute the given values:
\[
k_1 = 10^{-1} \, \text{s}^{-1}
\]
\[
k_2 = 10^4 \, \text{s}^{-1}
\]
\[
T_1 = \frac{1}{0.0018} = 555 \, \text{K}
\]
\[
T_2 = \frac{1}{0.0012} = 833 \, \text{K}
\]
\[
R = 2 \, \text{cal K}^{-1} \text{mol}^{-1}
\]

\[
E_a = \frac{2 \ln \left( \frac{10^4}{10^{-1}} \right)}{1/555 - 1/833} = \frac{2 \ln (10^5)}{1/555 - 1/833} = \frac{2 \ln (10^5)}{1.81}
\]

\[
E_a = \frac{2 \times 15.80} {1.81} = 17.23 \, \text{kJ mol}^{-1}
\]

Thus, the activation energy is:
\[
