(b) How long does it take the proton to reach a speed of \(35,000 \, \text{m/s}\)?
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
(a) The acceleration of the proton is given by Newton's second law:  
\[
a = \frac{F}{m}
\]  
The force on the proton is due to the electric field:  
\[
F = qE = (1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C})(500 \, \text{N/C}) = 8.00 \times 10^{-17} \, \text{N}
\]  
Substituting into the acceleration equation:  
\[
a = \frac{8.00 \times 10^{-17} \, \text{N}}{1.67 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg}} = 4.80 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2
\]  
The magnitude of the acceleration is \(\boxed{4.80 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2}\).

(b) The acceleration is constant, so we use the kinematic equation:  
\[
v = u + at
\]  
The proton starts from rest, so \(u = 0\). Solving for \(t\):  
\[
t = \frac{v - u}{a} = \frac{35,000 \, \text{m/s}}{4.80 \times 10^{10} \, \text{m/s}^2} = 7.3 \times 10^{-4} \, \text{s}
\]  
The time it takes is \(\boxed{7.3 \times 10^{-4} \, \text{s}}\).
