The Gibbs free energy change for the reaction is given by:

\[ \Delta G = 2 \Delta G_f(\text{H}_2\text{O}(g)) - 2 \Delta G_f(\text{H}_2(g)) - \Delta G_f(\text{O}_2(g)} \]

Given the standard Gibbs free energy of formation for water (\( \Delta G_f(\text{H}_2\text{O}(g)) \)) as \(-237.1 \text{ kJ/mol}\), and the standard Gibbs free energy of formation for hydrogen (\( \Delta G_f(\text{H}_2(g)) \)) and oxygen (\( \Delta G_f(\text{O}_2(g)) \)) is zero (since they are in their standard states), we can calculate \(\Delta G\):

\[ \Delta G = 2(-237.1 \text{ kJ/mol}) - 2(0) - 0 = -474.2 \text{ kJ/mol} \]

This value is for 2 moles of hydrogen and 1 mole of oxygen to form 2 mole of water. Since the problem asks for the change in Gibbs free energy for the given amounts of reactants, the calculation is correct.

