","The lifetime of a particle is inversely proportional to its width in the energy spectrum, as given by the Heisenberg Uncertainty Principle:  
\[
\Delta E \cdot \Delta t \approx \hbar
\]  
where:  
- \(\Delta E\) is the energy uncertainty (width of the decay line),  
- \(\Delta t\) is the lifetime of the particle,  
- \hbar\) is the reduced Planck constant (\(1.0 \times 10^{-34}\) eV s).  

Rearranging the equation to solve for \(\Delta E\):  
\[
\Delta E \approx \frac{\hbar}{\Delta t}
\]  
Substituting the given values:  
\[
\Delta E \approx \frac{1.0 \times 10^{-34} \text{ eV s}}{2.0 \times 10^{-23} \text{ s}} = 5 \times 10^{-12} \text{ eV}
\]  
Convert \(\Delta E\) from eV to MeV:  
\[
\Delta E \approx 5 \times 10^{-12} \text{ eV} \times \frac{1 \text{ MeV}}{10^6 \text{ eV}} = 5 \times 10^{-18} \text{ MeV}
\]  
Thus, the width of the decay line is \(\boxed{5 \times 10^{-18} \text{ MeV}}\).  

Thus, the range of outcomes for measurements of the \(\Sigma^{+}\) rest energy would be:  
\[
\boxed{1385 \pm 5 \times 10^{-18} \text{ MeV}}
\]  

This means the measurement would range from \(\boxed{1385 \times 10^{-18} \text{ MeV}}\) to \(\boxed{1385 \times 10^{18} \text{ MeV}}\).  

