</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
The decay length (\(L\)) of an evanescent wave is given by:  
\[
L = \frac{\1}{k_0 (n_f - n_{\text{eff}})}.
\]  
where:  
- \(k_0 = \frac{2\pi}{c} = \frac{2\pi}{3 \times 10^8 \, \text{m/s}}\) is the free-space wavelength of the incident light,  
- \(n_f\) is the refractive index of the fiber fiber (GaAs),  
- \(n_{\text{eff}}\) is the effective refractive index of the waveguide mode.  

Given:  
- \(w = 1 \, \mu m = 10^{-6} \, \text{m}\)  
- \(n_{\text{eff}} - n_f \sim 0.3\)  

First, calculate \(k_0\):  
\[
k_0 = \frac{2\pi}{3 \times 10^8 \, \text{m/s}} \approx 6.64 \times 10^7 \, \text{rad/s}
\]  

Next, calculate the decay length \(L\):  
\[
L = \frac{1}{k_0 (n_f - n_{\text{eff}})} = \frac{1}{k_0 (0.3)} = \frac{1}{6.64 \times 10^7 \times 0.3} \approx 5.2 \times 10^{-8} \, \text{m} = 52 \, \mu m.
\]  

Thus, the decay length is \(\boxed{52 \, \mu m}\).
