To calculate the work done, we use the formula:  
\[ W = F \cdot d \cdot \cos \theta \]  
where:  
- \( W = 100.0 \, \text{J} \) (work done),  
- \( F = 60.0 \, \text{N} \) (force applied),  
- \( d = 10.0 \, \text{m} \) (distance traveled),  
- \( \cos \theta = 0.573 \) (cosine of the angle).  

Substituting the values:  
\[ W = 60.0 \, \text{N} \times 10.0 \, \text{m} \times cos 60.0° \]  
\[ W = 60.0 \times 10.0 \times 0.573 \]  
\[ W = 343.8 \, \text{J} \]  

Thus, the work done is \(\boxed{343.8 \, \text{J}}\).
