\]  
where:  
- \( I(\lambda, T) \) is the intensity of thermal radiation at wavelength \( \lambda \) and temperature \( T \).  
- \( \sigma \) is the Stefan-Boltzmann constant, approximately \( 5.67 \times 10^{-8} \, \text{W/m}^2 \text{K}^4 \).  
- \( c \) is the speed of light, approximately \( 3 \times 10^8 \, \text{m/s} \).  

This equation is derived from the Planck wavelength shift law, which relates the wavelength at which the intensity is maximum to the temperature:  
\[
\lambda_{\text{max}} = \frac{1}{c T} \left( \frac{1}{2\pi} \right)
\]  
This equation shows that the wavelength at which the black body emits the most radiation is inversely proportional to the temperature.  

The total power emitted by a black body is given by the Stefan-Boltzmann law:  
\[
P = \sigma A T^4
\]  
where \( A \) is the surface area of the black body.  

